      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nReplaces bare `assert not is_lora_enabled` statements in `Mamba2Model` and `Zamba2Model` constructors with descriptive `ValueError` exceptions when LoRA is enabled. Fixes #61850.\n\n## Claims\n\n- When `enable_lora=True` is provided for Mamba2 or Zamba2 models, initialization raises a descriptive `ValueError(\"enable_lora=True is not supported for Mamba2.\")` or `ValueError(\"enable_lora=True is not supported for Zamba2.\")` instead of an uninformative bare `AssertionError`.\n- Prevents silent bypass of LoRA checks when running Python with bytecode optimizations (`-O` / `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=1`).\n\n## Validation\n\n- Added unit tests in `tests/models/test_zamba2_mamba2_lora.py` validating that initializing `Mamba2Model` and `Zamba2Model` with an active `lora_config` raises `ValueError` matching the expected message.\n- Passed local linter checks: `ruff check` and `ruff format`.\n\n## Details\n\nIn `vllm/model_executor/models/mamba2.py` and `vllm/model_executor/models/zamba2.py`, LoRA support was guarded by a bare `assert not is_lora_enabled`. This resulted in:\n1. EngineCore dying with an opaque `AssertionError` with no explanation of why LoRA is unsupported or which model caused it.\n2. In production deployments running Python with `-O`, assertions are omitted entirely by the Python interpreter, which could lead to deeper undefined runtime failures.\n\nReplacing the bare assertion with an explicit `ValueError` makes the error actionable and robust across execution environments.\n\n---\n*Diff minimization and pre-flight validation performed with [ZeroSlop™ CI](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/zeroslop-by-ribbsaeter-systems) — [Ribbsaeter Systems](https://ribbsaetersystems.com/).*\n",
